Wildcards — question mark super T, and P-E-C-S.

Question-mark-extends gave you a parameter you can read from. Its mirror image, question-mark-super-T, gives you one you can write to. And the mnemonic that ties the two together, P-E-C-S, is one of the most useful things to memorise in all of Java generics.

So, question-mark-super is a lower-bounded wildcard. A list of question-mark-super-integer means "a list of some unknown type that is integer, or a supertype of it" — so a list of integer, a list of number, and a list of object all qualify. And now the read-write trade-off flips. You can add integers, but you can only read elements back as Object. Picture an add-numbers method taking a list of question-mark-super-integer. You can add one, and add two — because whatever the list really is, an integer fits. But when you read index zero, it comes back only as an Object. Trying to read it as an integer is a compile error, because the list could actually be a list of number, not integer.

Why can you write? Because the list's element type is integer, or something more general, so an integer is always a valid element. And why can you only read as Object? Because you don't know how general it is — it might be a list of object — and the one type that everything is, is Object.

So here's the mental model. Where question-mark-extends is a producer, with data flowing out, question-mark-super is a consumer, with data flowing in — your method puts T's into it. You write to it; you don't meaningfully read from it.

And that gives the rule for choosing a wildcard on any parameter. P-E-C-S: Producer extends, Consumer super. If the parameter produces T's for you to read, use question-mark-extends-T. If the parameter consumes T's that you supply, use question-mark-super-T. And if it does both — you read and write — use an exact type, list of T, with no wildcard. The canonical signature that uses both at once is a copy. Static, angle-T, copy, taking a destination list of question-mark-super-T, and a source list of question-mark-extends-T; then, for each x in source, add x to destination. The source produces, so it's extends. The destination consumes, so it's super. Read that one signature, and P-E-C-S is doing the whole job.

And once you know to look, the pattern is everywhere in the standard library. Collections dot max takes a collection of question-mark-extends-T, because it reads the elements — a producer, so extends. For-each takes a consumer of question-mark-super-T, because you hand each element to the consumer — a consumer, so super. A comparator of question-mark-super-T, on sort and tree-set, consumes your elements. And stream dot map takes a function of question-mark-super-T, to question-mark-extends-R — it consumes a T and produces an R, so it's P-E-C-S on both ends of one function.

So the rule of thumb, whenever you design a generic method: ask of each parameter, do I read from it, or write to it? Read, use question-mark-extends. Write, use question-mark-super. Both, use the exact type. Apply P-E-C-S, and your APIs accept the widest possible range of caller types, while staying fully type-safe — the difference between a method that's a pleasure to call, and one that rejects every list you hand it. And all of this works because of a single run-time reality: the compiler is doing bookkeeping that mostly vanishes once the program runs. That reality is type erasure — which is next.
